{
  "_": "Agent harness rules for this repo. Read by ~/.claude/loops/*, whose source lives in harness/loops/ HERE — so this file doubles as the worked example the harness README describes. Everything is optional; omitted keys fall back to harness_rules.py DEFAULTS, which are the safe end of every switch. path/github/default_branch are NOT settable — they are detected from the checkout.",
  "_migration": "This file was TRACKED as `.harness-rules` until it split in two. The tracked half is policy and lives here; the untracked half holds only what one MACHINE's providers will actually serve (reviewers.<seat>.{enabled,model,effort}, and narrowing only) — see harness_rules.py. That half is read from TWO places and the repo's wins per key: `$QUARTERBACK_HARNESS_RULES` or `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/quarterback/harness-rules.json` for the whole box (prefer this — it is read by every repo and every worktree on the machine, so a fresh worktree is correct the moment it exists), and `.harness-rules` beside this file for this checkout alone. Migrating a repo is one commit: `git mv .harness-rules .harness-rules.sample`. Two things to know. (1) If YOUR checkout had local edits to the old tracked file, copy it aside before pulling that commit — the rename deletes the tracked path, and the copy is what becomes your per-box overlay, which is gitignored from then on. (2) If a repo does the two halves in two commits, both files end up tracked: that is safe, the sample wins as the baseline and the resolver names the file it did not read, and `git rm --cached .harness-rules` finishes it (untracking the file while keeping it on disk, where it is now the overlay).",
  "_two_refs": "Unattended runs (the systemd timer) read this file from `origin/<default branch>`, never from the working tree, so a poisoned PR cannot rewrite the rules governing its own review. The untracked overlay is part of the working tree whoever put it there — code run from a PR checkout can create it — so it is read on the INTERACTIVE path only.",

  "mode": {
    "name": "cleanroom",
    "_": "How this repo is worked (#178). The unit of work is a GitHub issue: claim it, take your OWN worktree with `create-worktree`, land through a PR. The other mode is `jungle` — shared checkout, commits straight to the branch, structure carried on the board instead of in issues and PRs — which is how 65lowther is worked and is not a lesser way to work.",
    "_declaring_it_is_not_a_no_op": "`cleanroom` is also the built-in default, so it would be easy to read this key as decoration. It is not. `qb-mode` distinguishes a repo that DECLARED cleanroom from one that merely inherited it, and warns on thinner evidence about the first: being in the primary checkout is enough on its own. An undeclared repo is given the benefit of the doubt until the checkout is one that actually hands out worktrees, because it might be somebody's private clone that no second agent will ever open. Writing this line is what turns the guard on for this repo.",
    "_why_it_is_stated_here": "This repo has broken its own rule twice with nothing to stop it. 2026-08-17: three agents in one checkout, and a `nix build` compiled one agent's in-progress edits as another's evidence — caught only by diffing two derivations. 2026-08-25: four agents in the same checkout, and a `git reset --hard` typed by one destroyed another's uncommitted review fixes. Neither time did anyone CHOOSE to work in the shared tree; the session simply started there and nothing said this repo is not worked that way. `qb-mode` exits 3 in this checkout now, and the session-start hook says so before any work begins. The mode is read from `origin/<default branch>` and never from the working tree, so an edit in front of you — uncommitted, or on a branch that has not landed — cannot turn the warning off; policy for a shared checkout is not something one branch decides for everybody standing in it.",
    "_axes": "`isolation` (worktree|shared) and `landing` (pr|direct) are settable on their own and both are omitted here, which takes them from the preset. They exist because the two dials are independent even though they move together today: a repo that wanted its own worktrees but direct commits would say \"landing\": \"direct\" and still be a cleanroom tree."
  },

  "reviewers": {
    "_": "claude stays on the default `opus`, a floating alias that always resolves to the current model and so cannot rot. The other three are pinned, because their slugs are versioned build names.",

    "codex": {
      "model": "gpt-5.6-luna",
      "effort": "max",
      "_": "Pinned rather than left on the CLI's own default, which is whatever that install happens to ship and therefore differs between boxes — the panel is a controlled comparison, and an unpinned seat makes 'codex found more than claude' unattributable to a model.",
      "_deliberate_override": "This pin argues WITH the DEFAULTS comment in harness_rules.py, which declines to pin codex globally on the grounds that a slug is a versioned build name that gets retired and takes a whole vendor with it. That reasoning is right for the fleet default and is overridden here on purpose: this repo IS the panel's test bench, so an unattributable comparison costs it more than a rotted pin, and the pin's failure is loud (see _precondition) rather than a silently thinner review. The liability is real and per-box — a machine on codex < 0.144 loses the seat entirely until the pin is cleared — so the answer when that happens is to bump the slug or drop this key, not to wonder why codex went quiet. Verified on one machine on one date; treat it as a claim about that box, not about the fleet.",
      "_effort": "luna accepts low|medium|high|xhigh|max but NOT ultra, so `max` is the top of its range. Spelled `model_reasoning_effort` via `-c` on this CLI, `--thinking` on pi — same config key here, different word there.",
      "_precondition": "Needs codex >= 0.144; an older CLI is refused by the API with a 400 'requires a newer version of Codex'. That failure is loud rather than silent — `is_rejection` short-circuits the retries and the report names the model that was refused — so a rotted pin costs a reviewer visibly. Verified 2026-08-15 on codex-cli 0.147.0: the exact panel argv (`codex exec … --model gpt-5.6-luna -c model_reasoning_effort=max`) returned a well-formed findings array at exit 0."
    },

    "antigravity": {
      "enabled": true,
      "model": "gemini-3.7-flash-high",
      "_": "Third vendor. The DEFAULTS keep this seat off, because `agy` is a workstation package authenticating against a personal Google account and so never reaches a headless box; it is on HERE because this repo is developed on those workstations and a third vendor is worth having. Host scope is enforced by where the package lives, not by this switch: panel.py skips any reviewer whose CLI is not on PATH, so a headless box records `antigravity: CLI absent` and runs the panel without it.",
      "_headless_cost": "That skip used to cost more than a seat. `coverage_veto` turned every reviewer with `ran: False` into a veto line, and `round_stop` computes `confident` as `not veto`, so two permanently-absent seats made a confident stop unreachable on exactly the boxes that run unattended — a real signal converted into constant noise. Fixed in panel.py rather than by scoping this switch: a reviewer whose CLI is not installed is a fact about the HOST, not about the round, so it is reported without vetoing. That absence is recorded as state on the reviewer's run (`ReviewerRun.absent`), not inferred from the wording of the skip line. Every other way of not running still vetoes, and so does a round in which NO reviewer ran at all.",
      "_model": "Pinned to a slug `agy models` actually lists, and confirmed by RUNNING it — 2026-08-15, the exact panel argv (`agy --mode plan -p … --model gemini-3.7-flash-high`) returned a well-formed findings array at exit 0. Name resolution, model availability and remaining quota are three different questions, and only a real run answers the last two.",
      "_argv_ceiling": "This seat has a SECOND standing cost, and it is exempted for the same reason as the first (#113). `agy` is the only reviewer whose prompt travels in argv \u2014 it cannot read one off stdin \u2014 so the kernel caps what it can be handed at ARGV_PROMPT_MAX_BYTES, and on a large diff it structurally cannot see all of it: measured on PR #160, 116,771 of 175,547 chars, 66.5%. That is a fact about this BOX and this diff size, true every round, so `coverage_veto` reports the truncation without spending the round's confidence on it. Recorded as state (`reviewers.antigravity.argv_capped` in the payload), and it takes BOTH halves: `argv_clamp` says the kernel is what cut the budget, and the composed material says the seat actually lost some of the review target. Truncation by a `max_diff_chars` this box could have carried is a number somebody typed, is fixable, and still vetoes \u2014 and so does a clamp that shrinks the prompt to nothing, because a seat handed no diff reviewed nothing. The floor: if every seat that ran was cut this way, the veto stands.",
      "_no_effort": "`effort` is deliberately unset. agy bakes the reasoning level into the slug — `agy models` lists gemini-3.7-flash-high / -medium / -low as three separate models — so an `--effort` on top would be a second, conflicting knob for the same thing.",
      "_not_gemini_cli": "This is `agy` (Antigravity), NOT gemini-cli: a different CLI on a different endpoint. lexray dropped `gemini-3.7-flash` from its gemini-cli seat because every call 429'd on an exhausted free-tier bucket shared with 3.5. That finding does not transfer to this seat, and the control run above is what says so rather than the assumption."
    },

    "pi": {
      "enabled": true,
      "model": "openrouter/moonshotai/kimi-k3",
      "effort": "high",
      "_": "Fourth vendor, off in the DEFAULTS for the same host reason as antigravity and on here for the same repo reason. It is the only member reaching outside the three US frontier labs — claude, codex and antigravity are trained similarly enough to agree for the same reasons, so Kimi is the one seat that can disagree for a different one. That is the whole point of a diverse panel.",
      "_model": "Fully qualified because pi fronts many providers and openrouter is the only one authenticated here (`pi auth check --provider openrouter` -> ready, 2026-08-15). A bare slug would be sent to an unauthenticated default. Verified with the exact panel argv, same run as above.",
      "_effort": "Maps to pi's `--thinking`, whose set (off|minimal|low|medium|high|xhigh|max) is NOT codex's — see EFFORTS in panel.py. Same config key, different word on each CLI.",
      "_cost": "The only METERED member: the other three ride subscriptions, this one bills per token through OpenRouter. It is the seat to look at first if the OpenRouter bill surprises you.",
      "_blast_radius": "Stated because a committed `enabled: true` on a metered seat is a standing charge, not a per-invocation choice. It bills on EVERY panel run of this repo on a box that carries `pi` — including the unattended lander and epic loops, and once per round of a multi-round fix cycle, which re-runs the whole panel each round. That is the intended scope: this repo is the panel's test bench and a fourth vendor outside the US frontier labs is what it is for. The brake for a run that should not pay it is `panel.py --reviewers claude,codex`, which overrides this file for that run; the brake for a rate that stops being worth it is flipping this key.",
      "_hosts": "Same story as antigravity — `pi` is a workstation package, and a box without it records `pi: CLI absent` rather than failing the panel."
    },

    "sonarqube": {
      "enabled": false,
      "_": "No SonarCloud project covers this repo, so the panel runs with no hard gate and every finding is a soft one judged on its merits. Stated explicitly rather than omitted: the default is off too, but a reader of this file should not have to guess whether the absence was a decision or an oversight."
    }
  },

  "review_panel": {
    "reviewer_code_access": true,
    "_reviewer_code_access": "Left at the default, and stated anyway, because this is the one governance key whose right value depends on WHO opens the PRs. On, because this repo's contributors are the fleet's own agents \u2014 they already hold a shell on this box as this user, so a hostile AGENTS.md smuggled into a reviewer's cwd is not a door any of them needs. The convention-file strip is a DENYLIST and will rot as vendors add files; that is the accepted cost here, and it is exactly why a repo taking outside contributions sets this false instead. #52's epic, which points the panel at PRs that are not ours, is the first caller expected to want it off.",
    "_reviewer_code_access_reach": "It buys ONE seat today. Only claude can be told 'read but do not execute' (--allowedTools Read Grep Glob, no Bash) and it enforces a working-directory boundary of its own; codex's -c knobs only REMOVE tools and its single read path is the shell, so granting reads grants execution (against #92) and re-opens the tool-hunt that once spent 99% of a run; pi's --no-tools is all-or-nothing over read/bash/edit/write; antigravity has no tool mechanism at all. So codex, pi and antigravity keep the empty sandbox, and their could_not_assess entries stay exempt from the coverage veto while claude's count again. That split is recorded per round in the payload (code_access.seats) \u2014 a seat that can read the tree while another cannot is a bigger confound than an unpinned model.",
    "fix_severity_floor": "P3",
    "round_trigger_floor": "P2",
    "low_severity_fix_lines": 40,
    "max_fix_growth": 3.0,
    "max_fix_growth_chars": 30000,
    "reviewer_scope": "diff",
    "fixer_may_defer": true,
    "file_deferral_issues": "P2",
    "require_failing_test": false,
    "max_rounds": 2,
    "escalate_on": {"premise_repeated": 2, "fix_injection": 0.5},
    "budget": {"tokens_per_day": null, "runs_per_day": null, "tokens_per_pr": null, "runs_per_pr": null, "fleet_tokens_per_day": null},
    "budget_window_hours": 24,
    "distant_merge_lines": 20,
    "_278_distant_merge_lines": "#278's dial, at its DEFAULTS value and written out anyway like the eight above. After an integration merge the reviewer asks how much of the merge is genuinely new material to THIS PR: git diff between the commit the round read and the merge result, restricted to the files this PR touches, counted in changed lines. At or under 20 the merge is DISTANT and the round that ran before it still stands (nothing is claimed as reviewed that was not \u2014 the merged code is not this PR's change); past it the merge is INVOLVED and its resolution is unreviewed work that gets reviewed, only it and not the whole PR again. Lines rather than file overlap or hunk overlap: file overlap is a hair-trigger that would send every base merge back to a full re-review, hunk overlap cannot be read the same way from GitHub's compare API and from a local git diff so the two ends of the feature would disagree, and lines is continuous \u2014 which is what makes a dial mean something rather than rename a boolean. 20 and at the LOW end deliberately: reading INVOLVED when the merge was distant buys one scoped round, while reading DISTANT when it was involved ships a hand-resolved merge nobody read, which is #80's stderr_gist incident \u2014 a landed fix silently reverted because a function that had MOVED met a main that already had it, git conflicting on neither. This repo is where that happened, so it runs the shipped default. null is the off switch (every head move is a review of earlier code, the pre-#278 behaviour); 0 admits only a resolution that is empty over this PR's own files. A range with no merge commit in it is never distant whatever its size \u2014 that is a push, and unreviewed work of this PR's own kind holds at any size.",
    "_165": "The seven dials from #165, every one of them left at its DEFAULTS value and written out anyway \u2014 this repo IS the panel's test bench and the measurement that set these numbers was taken here, so a reader of this file should not have to open harness_rules.py to find out what policy the panel ran under. That measurement, across the seven PRs panelled on 2026-08-16: the last round of each raised 201 findings no earlier round had, 128 of them (63.7%) created by the fix pass immediately before it, against a ~7% industry baseline for bad-fix injection. All seven panels ended on the round cap. Nine of this repo's open issues are the panel's own deferred-finding overflow.",
    "_165_floors": "fix_severity_floor and round_trigger_floor are two keys because they are two questions. As of 2026-08-22 this repo answers them P3/P2, which is the shipped DEFAULTS split, restored here after two days at P2/P2.\n\nWHY THEY WERE BOTH P2, and it was right at the time. Measured 2026-08-20 across the four PRs then open: 132 master-confirmed findings, 5 P1 and 32 P2. A P2 floor dropped 95 of 132 (72.0%) and lost no P1. Every one of those four PRs had already ended on the round cap with its findings handed to nobody (#42), so the P3 tier was not being fixed in the round already open \u2014 the premise the P3 default rests on. It was being deferred into an issue nobody would action: 15 such issues, ~290 findings, 15% of the open backlog. A floor that fixes P3s in practice beats one that files them, and this repo was doing the latter.\n\nWHY P3 IS BACK. The condition this key set for itself was 'restore P3/P2 the day the deferred-finding backlog is empty'. That is NOT the condition being met \u2014 the backlog is still there and is deliberately being left until the loop works. What changed is the other half of the argument. The reason a P3 floor could not simply be kept was that fixing that tier ACCUMULATES: #188's 185-line feature became 721 churned lines, 74% of it review-response code, off a round-2 list that was 89% below P2. On 2026-08-21 both halves of that shipped \u2014 low_severity_fix_lines (#297) budgets the band cheapest-first and counts rather than estimates, and max_fix_growth (#298) was measuring a whole-PR baseline against ONE ROUND'S INCREMENT, so a PR at 3.90x cleared a 3.0x cap and the backstop never fired. Both ends are whole-PR sizes now.\n\nSo the class this floor exists for \u2014 correctness expressed as craft, a missing regression test on a parser or an auth boundary, a missing timeout, a migration rollback gap \u2014 is reachable again without the accumulation that made it unaffordable. This is an EXPERIMENT with a stated way back: if the fix pass is again most of the PR, the budget is the first thing to tighten, and P2/P2 with the reasoning above is the known-good fallback.",
    "_482_deferral_issues": "#482's dial, at its DEFAULTS value and written out like the ones above it. Which deferrals get a GitHub ISSUE as well as the board row every deferral gets anyway. The two records were being conflated: the board row chains by finding key across rounds, feeds /panel and is what stops the leaderboard scoring a confident wrong finding like a real one; the GitHub issue is a work item on a human's tracker. Those coincide for a P1 or P2 deferral and do not for the P3/P4 tail, and the tail is where the volume is.\\n\\nMEASURED HERE, 2026-08-26. Roughly twenty open issues in this repo are the panel's own deferred-finding exhaust and nothing else: #66 #69 #72 #74 #95 #104 #111 #119 #120 #126 #132 #133 #140 #223 #237 #285 #286 #288 #300. Every one is a capped or below-floor round with nowhere to put what was left, and #283 is a rescue FROM one of them - three live defects that had been sitting inside a deferred-findings dump nobody read. At that volume the tracker stops being a queue and starts being where findings go to not be found, and each one dilutes the ranking #435's queue and the drainer (#476) exist to produce. Rich, the same day: 'the board itself should hold the dumping ground of fiddly P3 and P4 issues we have left lying around.'\\n\\nP2 KEEPS THE ONES THAT ARE REAL WORK ITEMS on the tracker and leaves the tail as rows. Below the gate the finding still gets its deferred row, still carries a one-line note saying what it is and why it was not fixed, and is still relayed to the human - what it does not get is a second copy on a backlog. deferred_to is nullable, the API accepts a deferred outcome without one, and /panel renders a targetless row rather than treating it as broken; that was #482's open question and it is settled with a test rather than an assumption.\\n\\nalways restores the pre-#482 behaviour exactly, never files none at all, and an escalation is exempt at EVERY setting - its issue asks a question rather than filing a task, and it is what carries that question past the end of the session.",
    "_297_budget": "low_severity_fix_lines (#297) is the churned lines a round may spend on findings between fix_severity_floor and round_trigger_floor \u2014 at P3/P2, exactly the P3 band. Spent cheapest-first, counted with git diff --numstat after the fact rather than forecast before it, and the remainder is reported and recorded rather than dropped.\n\nIT IS LIVE AS OF 2026-08-22. It shipped inert on 2026-08-21, when this repo had both floors at P2 and the band was empty; restoring fix_severity_floor to P3 (see _165_floors) is what it was built for and what turns it on. A budget rather than a per-fix cap because #188's round 1 was not one balloon \u2014 it was 408 lines of individually reasonable small fixes, which no per-fix cap can see. 40 is the shipped default and has never been exercised here; if the P3 tier starts accumulating again this is the first number to move, before the floor.",
    "_165_growth": "max_fix_growth is the backstop for the case the floors cannot reach: a fix pass that has ALREADY written a second change. On PR #236 three rounds went 18 -> 25 -> 24 findings with 19 of round 3's 24 attributed by the panel to the preceding fix pass, the PR grew from 359 to 2,313 insertions, none of the 67 findings was in the bug fix it existed for, and the last fix pass introduced an unbounded hang (read_bytes() on a FIFO, with the guard against exactly that dropped). It landed at 62 insertions. 3.0 is deliberately loose \u2014 a genuine fix that adds the tests the review asked for can double a small diff, and that is not this shape. `null` here is the off switch, which is the one place in this file where null does not mean 'inherit'.",
    "_492_growth_chars": "max_fix_growth_chars is the ABSOLUTE half of that ceiling (#492), at its DEFAULTS value and written out like the dials above it. The multiple alone hands its rope out in proportion to the starting size: at 3.0x a 113-line PR may grow ~226 lines and a 2,000-line one may grow 4,000, so the loosest allowance goes to the case most in need of a ceiling. The cycle stops on whichever of the two is crossed FIRST, which means the pair can only ever tighten \u2014 nothing it lets through would have been caught by the multiple alone.\n\nCHARS AND NOT LINES, and the unit is in the name. max_fix_growth divides pr_chars by pr_chars, and two halves of one ceiling read off two different measurements is #298's defect one level up. 30,000 is roughly 380-450 churned lines at PR #188's own measured 66 chars a churned line; it stops #188's growth of 536 lines and #236's of 1,954, with margin, and does NOT stop the 113-line cycle in #492, which grew ~122 lines \u2014 correctly, since that is the 'binds a round late' half of the report and guard_ratio is the earlier signal filed for it.\n\nA SECOND KEY rather than a two-part max_fix_growth value: BOARD_DIALS types this dial as a scalar number, the board stores one JSON value per dial, and null is already the off switch for max_fix_growth \u2014 a pair would have to answer which half a bare null switched off. Two keys, two nulls, and null on BOTH is the pre-#165 behaviour of no growth check at all. A repo that wrote max_fix_growth: null before this landed is told in config_notes that the other half is still in force.",
    "_165_max_rounds": "Two, as of 2026-08-22, restored from one. Both settings and the argument between them are kept here because this key has now been moved in both directions on evidence.\n\nWHY IT WAS CUT TO ONE on 2026-08-20: all seven panels in #165's measurement ended on the cap, and so did all four PRs open that day. A round cap that is always reached is not a safety net, it is a budget \u2014 and the findings it stops at are recorded as unactioned debt rather than fixed. The trade was explicit: a fix written in round 1 ships without another panel reading it, with max_fix_growth left as the only guard against that fix being a second change.\n\nWHY IT IS TWO AGAIN. Round 2 is where this repo's own evidence says the expensive defects are found \u2014 it caught the FIFO hang that round 1's fix created on PR #236, and on 2026-08-21 PR #299 ran five rounds in which 39 of 53 findings after round 1 were introduced by the previous fix pass, round 2 being 17 of 17. A cycle that cannot re-read its own fix cannot see that at all.\n\nWhat makes a second round affordable now is that three things landed on 2026-08-21 that did not exist when it was cut. escalate_on.premise_repeated (#84) stops a cycle the SECOND time a fix is written against a premise the previous round invalidated \u2014 and note that brake CANNOT FIRE AT max_rounds 1, because there is no second fix pass to refuse, so one is the setting that switches it off. low_severity_fix_lines (#297) bounds what round 1's fix pass may accumulate, which was the actual complaint behind the cap. And max_fix_growth (#298) now measures the ratio it was always supposed to.\n\nThis is an EXPERIMENT and the way back is one key. If rounds again end on the cap with their findings becoming debt, one is the known-good value and the argument for it above is intact. panel.py --max-rounds still wins over this for a single run — unless the BOARD states max_rounds, in which case its value is a ceiling neither this file nor the flag may exceed (#55).",
    "_55_budget": "#55's spend ceiling, written out at ITS DEFAULTS VALUE — which is null on every key, meaning NO CEILING — for the same reason the dials above are: this repo is the panel's test bench and a reader should not have to open harness_rules.py to find out what governed a run. Nothing here is set, and that is deliberate: the mechanism landed with a default that changes nothing until a human writes a number.\n\nWHERE THE NUMBER BELONGS, AND IT IS NOT HERE. A ceiling a repo can raise from inside itself is decorative, so these five are board dials (`POST /dials`, human-only writes behind the Authelia edge) and the board's value beats whatever this file says. Writing one here is a self-imposed bound and is honoured when the board has stated none; it is not a way to raise one the board has stated.\n\nWHAT THE UNITS ARE. Tokens are input + output — `/review/stats`' own `billable`, since cached input is a slice OF input and reasoning sits inside output for some vendors and beside it for others. #15 landed the per-reviewer capture that makes that measurable, so the ceiling is denominated in the honest unit rather than in #55's crude reviews-per-day proxy. The RUN ceilings are not that proxy's leftovers: an uninstrumented seat (antigravity) and any run recorded before v2.14 report no tokens at all, so a token-only ceiling reads an unmeasured spend as no spend — and `runs_per_pr` is the only one of the five that binds a caller which renumbers its rounds, since a run is a row on the board whatever it called itself.\n\n0 is a real value and means nothing may be spent; null means no ceiling. `--force` does not override any of them.",
    "_55_budget_window_hours": "What \"per day\" means, in hours, for the two rolling ceilings. A dial rather than a constant because the window and the ceiling are one decision — halving the window halves the ceiling — and a fleet that could set only one of them would be setting a number whose meaning it could not see. The per-PR ceilings have no window at all: they are about one pull request's whole cost, and a rolling window would let a PR reviewed daily for a fortnight stay under a ceiling it passed on day two.",
    "_84_escalate_on": "#78's reserved matters, of which one is built: premise_repeated (#84), left at its DEFAULTS value and written out anyway for the same reason as the eight dials above. It is the futility brake — the second time a fix pass is written against one declared premise, the cycle stops and a human answers the premise. Evaluated when a fix is PROPOSED (`panel.py --premise`), not at the end of a round, because end-of-round is one whole fix pass and one whole panel too late. The measurement was taken here: PR #299, 2026-08-21, five rounds, of which rounds 1, 2 and 3 each found the previous round's fix reopening the same hole — patched through merge parents, then same-named refs, then a purely local branch — with the premise underneath all three (that a local repository can say where a release number LANDED) named at round 3 by a human. 39 of the 53 findings after round 1 were introduced by the previous fix pass; round 2 was 17 of 17. Note what max_rounds: 1 above means for it in THIS repo: one round is one fix pass, so a premise cannot repeat unless a caller raises the cap (`--rounds N`, which is exactly what #299 did). null is the off switch; quorum_failed and judge_absent are accepted and reported as unenforced.",
    "_489_fix_injection": "#489's injection gate, the block's second implemented matter, left at its DEFAULTS value and written out anyway like the dials above \u2014 this repo is the panel's test bench and the measurement behind the number was taken here. It is the fraction of a round's NEW outstanding findings that `_provenance` attributed to the fix pass immediately before it. Above 0.5 \u2014 more than half the round's news being the fix pass's own damage \u2014 the cycle ends, with a veto line and confident false. The rationale is that from round 2 what a round reviews IS the previous round's fix, so round_stop's rule 1 is fed by the loop's own output, and a termination test fed by its own output can only end on the cap.\n\nTHE INSTRUMENT CAME FIRST AND THIS IS THE CALIBRATION ARRIVING. panel.py withheld this gate deliberately and says so in as many words: #67 asks for the instrument before the gate, and a few dozen cycles of it are what would justify wiring it to anything. The cycles: 128 of 201 new findings across the seven PRs panelled on 2026-08-16 (see _165 above); 39 of 53 after round 1 on PR #299, its round 2 being 17 of 17 (see _84_escalate_on); 64% then 87% on the cycle #489 was filed from, over a PR whose actual change was 113 lines. Every one of those is far above 0.5 and every one ran to its cap.\n\nWHAT IS STILL NOT CALIBRATED is where a HEALTHY cycle sits, which is the number that decides the false-positive rate \u2014 so read this as an EXPERIMENT with a one-key way back. What makes it affordable is that the rule can only ever turn a `go again` into a STOP: a dry round, a below-floor policy stop and a round holding an escalation all keep the reason and the confidence they earned, so no value of this can make a review look cleaner than it is. And under max_rounds 2 the only round it can fire on is the one the cap would have ended anyway, which means what it buys this repo today is a better stop reason and one more veto line rather than an earlier finish. It bites when a caller raises the cap \u2014 `--max-rounds 5`, which is exactly what #299 did.\n\nA THRESHOLD ON THIS NUMBER ERRS SAFE. _provenance documents its own bias: `introduced` requires exact membership in the fix pass's added lines and cannot see a defect the fix introduced by DELETING a guard, so the count is a floor rather than a measurement. A measured 0.64 is at least 0.64. The unattributable buckets sit in the denominator for the same reason \u2014 they depress the rate, so a round the harness could not place does not end a cycle. null (or false) is the off switch; 1 and above are REFUSED rather than clamped, because a rate is at most 1.0 and the comparison is strict, so such a value would be the brake off behind a number that reads as armed. The minimum denominator is panel_rounds.FIX_INJECTION_MIN_NEW (4) and is a constant, not a second dial: a rate over two findings is not a rate.",
    "_165_require_failing_test": "False, and it is the one key here that enforces nothing. #165 argues the evidence contract is the real termination condition \u2014 a finding ships a reproducible failing test or it is an observation \u2014 and it needs an artefact nobody emits: #92's standing decision is that a reviewer never gains an execution capability (it EMITS a test, CI or the fixer runs it) and #114 requires the test be shown RED against the unfixed code. Turning it on here would be recorded, reported and inert, and the round says so in config_notes. The key exists so the work has a home and this repo can opt in the day it is built."
  },
  "in_flight": {
    "max": null,
    "min": null,
    "_": "#337's bound on concurrent work, AT ITS DEFAULT, which is no bound at all. Written out rather than omitted for the same reason as sonarqube and preland.disabled_checks: null is also the built-in default, and a reader should not have to guess whether the absence was a decision. It is one \u2014 this repo is where the fan-out was measured (eight agents against one main on 2026-08-22: two branches minted migration 0029 independently, a third was renumbered twice mid-flight, the largest open diff went DIRTY the moment the first landed) and it is deliberately still unbounded, because the machinery ships before the number does. Set `max` here when somebody is around to watch what a throttled fleet does.",
    "_max": "The ceiling: how many units of work may be in flight in THIS repo at once, fleet-wide. The count is claims \u2014 `GET /claims/in-flight` counts live `work` claims naming an issue or a PR here, whoever holds them \u2014 and it is enforced at the checkout, by `qb-admit`, which `create-worktree` calls before it takes the claim. A full window REFUSES the checkout, the same refusal `--require-claim` already makes when the issue is taken, at the same moment, for a second reason. Admission, not queueing: the item stays on the plan, unclaimed and visibly waiting. 0 is a legal value and means admit nothing, which is a freeze rather than a typo; a value that is neither null nor a non-negative integer is reported and NOT enforced, because failing open on a malformed ceiling is the safer of the two failures for a fleet nobody is watching.",
    "_min": "The floor, and NOTHING READS IT. It exists so the planner's discretion (#232) has a configured home when it is built: a ceiling with no floor starves throughput the moment everything queued is genuinely disjoint, and `disjoint` is a judgement that needs the changed-file overlap (#101/#287) and a planner that does not exist yet. Recorded, reported, inert \u2014 the shape `review_panel.require_failing_test` already has here.",
    "_not_here": "Four things #337 asks for that this key deliberately does NOT carry yet, so nobody reads the block as more governed than it is. The ceiling is ADVISORY: `qb-admit` counts and then `create-worktree` claims, so two checkouts starting in the same second both see room and the window can be exceeded by however many raced \u2014 making it exact means moving admission into the board so the count and the insert are one transaction, which is not a change to ship alongside a bound that is off. The planner's discretion inputs \u2014 merge-conflict risk, blast radius \u2014 are absent. So is the migration slot: `kind=migration, key=<repo>` is the same claim primitive and probably wants a ceiling of 1, and whether it is a separate slot or a heavy weighting inside this window is open. And `--no-claim` remains the way to take a checkout outside the count entirely, which is consistent (outside qb is outside qb) and is warned about loudly at the checkout rather than allowed silently."
  },

  "preland": {
    "disabled_checks": [],
    "_": "Every check preland.py can detect, it runs here. Stated explicitly rather than omitted, for the same reason as sonarqube and epic.sub_pr_merge below: the default is an empty list too, and a reader should not have to guess whether the absence was a decision. It was.",
    "_the_one_worth_naming": "`review` is the check a repo would be tempted to disable, because it HOLDs when the board cannot be read at all — an unset QUARTERBACK_BASE_URL is not evidence the PR is clean, it is the absence of evidence either way. This repo IS the board, is enrolled on it, and records every panel run there, so turning it off would be turning off the only check that reads what the panel actually said. If a future box cannot reach the board, the answer is to fix the box or disable it for THAT checkout, not to commit the switch here."
  },

  "issue_pickup": {
    "enabled": false,
    "only_labels": [],
    "allowed_authors": ["prisonblues"],
    "require_human_triage": true,
    "skip_labels": ["needs-human/*"],
    "skip_when_unlabelled": true,
    "_": "What a loop may pick up of its OWN accord (#85, #86). Every value here is the default; they are written out rather than omitted because this is the block a reader will come to when they want to know whether anything on this repo can start work unprompted, and an empty block answers that with silence. The answer is no: `enabled` is false, and nothing turns it on implicitly. NOTE, since #277: this block governs what a loop ALREADY RUNNING may take of its own accord, and it is not the whole answer to \"can anything start work here unprompted\". Whether a session may be STARTED at all is `qb-start`, and that switch is per MACHINE (`programs.quarterback-harness.spawn.enable`) rather than per repo \u2014 deliberately, because it is a remote-execution primitive on the box rather than a policy about this codebase. It ships off too, and it is not a way round this gate: a spawned session is told a command and a number by whoever ran qb-start, and anything it then picks up on its own account comes back through here. Since #63 this block has a consumer: `issue_watch.py` reads a repo's open issues and says what each one is waiting on. Its SURVEY half needs no permission and runs either way \u2014 it chooses no work \u2014 but the action it names is gated here, and with this block as shipped that action is `none` for every issue.",
    "_allowed_authors": "Stated ahead of the gate it guards, on purpose. This repo is PUBLIC and anyone can open an issue; under #63's watcher that text becomes the instructions for an agent with a full shell on zeus. The mitigation has to be an allowlist and not a filter — a filter is a list of the phrasings somebody already thought of, and the attack is the one nobody thought of. Writing the allowlist now means turning the watcher on later is one switch rather than one switch plus a security decision made in the same hurried minute.",
    "_only_labels_is_still_empty": "Deliberately, and it is the second lock. Even with `enabled` flipped, nothing qualifies until this repo says what a pickup-able issue looks like. Turning the gate on is one decision; saying what may come through it is another.",
    "_require_human_triage": "The load-bearing line. An allowlist of labels authorises nothing if the agent can apply the labels — #78's `judge_model` problem one level out — so appetite.py reads the issue's label EVENTS and asks who applied it. NOTE, because it decides whether this setting is usable at all: nothing in harness/ writes labels today (`gh issue edit --add-label` appears nowhere), so the only thing that can open this gate is a person in the GitHub UI or typing `gh` by hand. That is the intended opener and not an oversight — but if a labelling agent is ever added, it MUST be named in `agent_actors` on the same commit, or this setting silently becomes decorative.",
    "_skip_labels": "#279's closed vocabulary (`needs-human/decision|taste|ui|environment|auth|other`), matched as a glob so the `other` escape hatch can grow the vocabulary a word without this list going stale by letting the new class through. #86 proposed design/ui/decision-owed/needs-scoping, written when this repo had no labels at all; these six exist and are already produced, and two vocabularies for one idea is the drift #65 has been paid for once."
  },

  "issue_filing": {
    "max_per_run": 1,
    "require_dedup_check": true,
    "unattended": false,
    "_": "How much a loop may FILE — the risk in the other direction (#85). Nine issues in one day, every one a response to something real, which is what makes it a risk rather than a bug: a loop applying the same standard with no ceiling produces a backlog nobody reads.",
    "_unattended": "Restates #40's standing decision as config, so a repo can relax it deliberately rather than by accident. An unattended run may still REPORT what it would have filed; that half was never the problem.",
    "_require_dedup_check": "Matters more than it looks. It is a search over the title's own words rather than an exact match, because the duplicate that matters is the one somebody phrased differently — an exact-title check would pass every time and read as a working gate."
  },

  "epic": {
    "sub_pr_merge": "gate",
    "_": "Whether the epic driver merges a reviewed sub-PR into the epic branch by itself, or holds each at a human merge. Stated explicitly for the same reason as sonarqube above — the default is `gate` too, and a reader should not have to guess whether that was a decision. It was.",
    "_why_off": "This is the switch that decides whether the merge gate merges anything, and that gate has now been wrong on its first attempt three rounds running: it read a zero exit as a review, then a push as a review, then the existence of a --json-file payload as a review. panel.py defeats all three — it exits 0, and writes a payload, on a title-pattern skip. The current fix reads the panel's own `reviewed`/`skip_reason`/`reviewers_ran` instead, which has no fourth proxy to get wrong; but 'we got it right this time' is what the previous three said, and defaulting closed makes a fourth mistake cost a printed line rather than an unreviewed merge.",
    "_when_to_flip": "When #78 lands `escalate_on` and `quorum` beside it, so the gate is governed rather than merely correct — and ideally when #77 can show that a confirmed finding survived contact with the code. Flipping it is one line and needs no code change."
  }
}
